#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069637} :activity/id #uuid "a5eebdd0-457e-5351-b479-fdec034c955a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049227 :topic/id #uuid "e5039159-d776-5d2a-8b00-bb798bc20935" :topic/math-academy-id 446} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070036 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the integral  $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2} \\textrm{d}x.$ Note that we have the repeated factor $(x+1)$ in the denominator. \n\nThe correct form of the partial fraction expansion of the integrand is\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2}=\\dfrac{A}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x+1)^2} \\,.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides the above equation by $(x+1)^2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =A(x+1)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-1)+2 & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+2 & =A(0+1)+B \\\\ 2 & =A+B \\\\ 2 & =A-1 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2}= \\dfrac{3}{(x+1)} -\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nNow this function can be integrated term by term. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+2}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{(x+1)}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}|x+1|+\\frac{1}{x+1}+C\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant of integration." :tutorial/id #uuid "de271889-71f4-5fa2-8346-7ed8fa03e000" :tutorial/math-academy-id 922 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d3ab3699-46bb-5348-b28d-d90ed53084d6" :step/math-academy-id 1800 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068587 :question/id #uuid "2fb19e4b-c455-599f-8856-5bee85f8efc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-508"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05dd17d8-4170-5392-89e7-7547184b8556" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050964}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332784}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two"} :step/id #uuid "a1f73cdf-ffed-50a9-b236-c48f569059a5" :step/math-academy-id 1801 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069724}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068747 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068588 :question/id #uuid "789c5233-a263-55a7-9b27-c834b2ceaf8d" :question/math-academy-id "e-509"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11647cdd-6315-5718-9425-88f4fb93faa7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534018}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor of Degree Higher Than Two"} :step/id #uuid "fac6b988-f49b-56f2-aa72-c550c04fafb0" :step/math-academy-id 1805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068799 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068642 :question/id #uuid "79049868-d542-5461-ab14-4a4336c9221d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7275"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf7f11d9-6aa8-5922-a5fa-89f7dba66644" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048375}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534098}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two That Require Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "e2a84f61-3405-5b4c-a923-cf909df72a37" :step/math-academy-id 26940 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069648}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069724 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068800 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068643 :question/id #uuid "d9f5cd99-436c-5089-a699-b9f9ff245157" :question/math-academy-id "e-7298"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68e983da-1f69-5be1-8034-9422a84ce6be" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533998}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor and Another Factor"} :step/id #uuid "08d4211a-dda1-5079-96e1-bb188234b652" :step/math-academy-id 27007 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069723}}] :activity/title "Integrating Rational Functions with Repeated Factors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068974}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534161} :activity/id #uuid "59250b6b-ada3-52c9-822b-a1bb62bb16a5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049227 :topic/id #uuid "e5039159-d776-5d2a-8b00-bb798bc20935" :topic/math-academy-id 446} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30ec5fc0-ee34-584e-9d88-01e89c37816f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533256}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533256} :answer-field/id #uuid "782cb243-75dd-5b8b-940c-de9dd816a45d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d58d6a00-0092-54a9-834c-43383db09b35" :question/math-academy-id "q-348615" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{5-3x}{{x}^{2}+2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5-3x}{{x}^{2}+2x+1}=\\frac{5-3x}{(x+1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5-3x}{(x+1)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+1}+\\frac{B}{(x+1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+1)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n5-3x=A(x+1)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-3(-1) & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ B & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-3(0) & =A(0+1)+8 \\\\ 5 & =A+8 \\\\ A & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5-3x}{(x+1)^{2}}=-\\frac{3}{x+1}+\\frac{8}{(x+1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{5-3x}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-2}(-\\frac{3}{x+1}+\\frac{8}{(x+1)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+8{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|\\,{|}_{-3}^{-2}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,{|}_{-3}^{-2} \\\\  & =-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-8(-1-(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-8(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "069d670c-cd83-5d0b-9f6b-dc9fcde46e21" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534113}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534113 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e9484e-2a02-53c8-af78-26814c64e2b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\ln 2+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533219}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533219} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfd3ac59-843e-571d-b47f-bc7b711b8324" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fd717c83-b2bc-5cde-b553-20c9ad00a74f" :question/math-academy-id "q-348616" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-4x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-4x+4}=\\frac{2x-1}{(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-1}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n2x-1=A(x-2)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)-1 & =A(2-2)+B \\\\ B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-1 & =A(0-2)+3 \\\\ -1 & =-2A+3 \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-1}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{2x-1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(\\frac{2}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{(x-2)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|\\,{|}_{3}^{4}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)-3(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{1})-3(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "288cf9b3-dd3f-5505-b4fa-8e8060a38d1d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534131 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc4ab644-7ab1-52fe-acea-608b6283d2d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\ln 2+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533594}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533594} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5e49120-4a4d-5acc-b35e-098953907a6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4254f874-a296-5085-b2f1-6690320d6f45" :question/math-academy-id "q-346722" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-3}+\\frac{C}{(x-3)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x(x-3)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n9-2x=A(x-3)^{2}+Bx(x-3)+Cx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-2(0) & =A(0-3)^{2}+B(0)(0-3)+C(0) \\\\ 9 & =9A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-2(3) & =A(3-3)^{2}+B(3)(3-3)+C(3) \\\\ 3 & =3C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-2(1) & =A(1-3)^{2}+B(1)(1-3)+C(1) \\\\ 7 & =4A-2B+C \\\\ 7 & =4(1)-2B+1 \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x-3}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-(-1+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "67e1f938-d404-58a9-83d5-e63e0a59f2a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534102}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534140 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534018 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b1f2cc0-8a4e-5ea4-a72e-cd65516b5d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|-\\frac{1}{(x+6)}+\\frac{2}{(x+6)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533316} {:answer/id #uuid "7d22e668-822b-5060-b1a5-9f37a1e7fced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|+\\frac{4}{(x+6)}+\\frac{1}{2(x+6)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533437} {:answer/id #uuid "972c848e-af74-5c16-9e1d-640235fc2342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|+\\frac{4}{(x+6)}-\\frac{3}{2(x+6)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533509} {:answer/id #uuid "a747ce04-2a78-5a1f-92c1-42721d3c067e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(x+6)}-\\frac{1}{2(x+6)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533544} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d45bcb-56e5-5650-bf20-a80f6e1fdbca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|+\\frac{2}{(x+6)}+\\frac{1}{(x+6)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533655}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533437} :answer-field/id #uuid "47ba250e-6800-54ee-b70a-058d44137afb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6d6e949e-2c23-5f49-968a-1d0b9baadd85" :question/math-academy-id "q-115659" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+11}{(x+6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+11}{(x+6)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x+6}+\\frac{B}{(x+6)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+6)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+6)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+8x+11=A(x+6)^{2}+B(x+6)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-6,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6)^{2}+8(-6)+11 & =A(-6+6)^{2}+B(-6+6)+C \\\\ -1 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}+8(0)+11 & =A(0+6)^{2}+B(0+6)+C \\\\ 11 & =36A+6B-1 \\\\ 36A+6B & =12 \\\\ 6A+B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-5$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5)^{2}+8(-5)+11 & =A(-5+6)^{2}+B(-5+6)+C \\\\ -4 & =A+B-1 \\\\ A+B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the equations $6A+B=2$ and $A+B=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5A & =2-(-3) \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =-3 \\\\ B & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+11}{(x+6)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x+6}-\\frac{4}{(x+6)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{(x+6)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+11}{(x+6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x+6}-\\frac{4}{(x+6)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{(x+6)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+6}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{(x+6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{(x+6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|+\\frac{4}{(x+6)}+\\frac{1}{2(x+6)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."} :step/id #uuid "a1dbcc12-54b2-55ac-bd69-f6c875fd3cf4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534131}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534161 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043d1ce5-9e3e-5519-9a4c-03569c147664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\frac{1}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247455} {:answer/id #uuid "554d505f-19eb-54e9-8916-dac052fc21dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248193} {:answer/id #uuid "64b65e65-4617-5e48-b1cf-a0372e6395b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{3}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248333} {:answer/id #uuid "7379ce95-99ed-5b20-9302-b338646b620c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{2}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{2(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248436} {:answer/id #uuid "d091e770-03db-512b-a3b7-40a0f2053ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248193} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aca0b8a-ad9d-5b96-a5a6-ae937ea506b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "513d3507-74c5-5ea9-ac09-4c99e11dedbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-115623" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{2x+1}+\\frac{B}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(2x+1)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(2x+1)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}=A(2x+1)^{2}+B(2x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =A{(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)}^{2}+B(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+C \\\\ 1 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2} & =A(2(0)+1)^{2}+B(2(0)+1)+C \\\\ 0 & =A+B+1 \\\\ A+B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-1)^{2} & =A(2(-1)+1)^{2}+B(2(-1)+1)+C \\\\ 4 & =A-B+1 \\\\ A-B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=3$ and $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =3-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =-1 \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{2}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{2}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."} :step/id #uuid "fd6a53a2-cedf-57ed-83ba-72d0485606e6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534140}}] :activity/title "Integrating Rational Functions with Repeated Factors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533122}]}
